#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "9b188b17-3d3f-5063-b12b-cdc6b58191d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-339" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/e3/e3feb58766bbbb4dc2ed0cd738c7a5d74d31a6d3bbc2e210010b9e5ecbc21da8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=-1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27cfed2793c78d0abaa91c4d5480724303ebf729fa22befbd6142e8e54039e64.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=-1$. Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and goes down to the right of $x=1$. Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) > 0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) < 0$. So, $x =1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/15/15842026cf3d019dcfd31c0f4f9aad54a4c8465dc1464feaa2d07b45e109ef74.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/0f/0f7e45db7621e8ecd7d915d49d14a8656070365023f12d7239c435f7da8f72fc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "dec0e17e-d9c6-5b6e-994f-717fb60b75fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-340" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/23/23c5620bdd59a6359eaa9bad6580b4b844237bead64b7138f7472b65c7e17c1b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points:\n\n$$\nx=1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=2\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2, \\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=2$. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=1$.Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$, the point at $x=2$ must be lower than the point at $x=1$.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e3a15ad7af8061bb800c5536fe054594402c7898ef7485a8a4165aff9cdc272d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=1$.Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(2,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=2$.Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 3$, we must have $f''(x) > 0$. So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,3)$. Also, since $f'(x)$ is constant and positive to the right of $x = 3$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing straight line on $(3,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/20/200f45be73f0c38d5cab4099ac83ab5d80022539e17cd0996f1ab0228a90a89e.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/16/16aff3b702623f3b6339cacfcdac4c9b43900e58f1228481d168b8b0535e1412.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100766 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "42f35d1e-5f81-5616-8d07-6c7bcf42d107" :question/math-academy-id "e-6929" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below. Given that $f(0)=0$, sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ef/ef91945c0b2261785677c4c8ba7e6d202f62bc6c2da20a213bcba03cbcad8556.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:\n$x=-2$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line $x=-2$.Since we are told that $f(0)=0$, we can draw that point on the graph.Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty)$, the point at $x=-2$ must be lower than the point at $x=0$.\n\n![](images/4b/4b599481e7a73ec38b6f0f627b1b9a551c65e6cd3875b3789f0e7ebfa03eb786.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical point:Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-\\infty,-2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-2$.\n\n![](images/1a/1ae0017fd07d6e9fa04f233ca31ab89a07260a5dbc5557e4c8ae35ff91d61f43.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/c2/c215907aa0cc156e92ab3b6710d844e774df3149656fc2f71f27ee01db87382e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100767 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b1fe80bf-85bd-590f-806b-1d22d818a696" :question/math-academy-id "e-6930" :question/problem "If $f'(x)= \\mid x+1 \\mid$, sketch a possible graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f'(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/57/57937f50c8adac57cd0a8b131ae2605466fe5e87e85ba324977a95b2f2c7a006.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x=-1$\n\nAlso, we have the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n- If $f'(x) < 0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{no extr.}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1$, as shown below.![](images/29/296d0c0e63bedc37841aa914e0fb07320fff634ea1f6ac019a84cc47b87966b4.png)\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and on $(-1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down the left of $x=-1$ and goes up to the right of $x=-1$. Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) < 0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) > 0$. So, $x =-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.So, we can draw as follows:![](images/1c/1c4fb55a07e1b55448ec2a2ada4fea07f363542cdf04a8676c90938b5c4f7fbe.png)\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)$:![](images/df/dfeb793809943dc496cc1f47b26f9b20aa3403b6d999787a7444ede605547226.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2eb6d5-ddb8-5d23-875a-5e2002482d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d25d30efea96fd63f923da67668ef77b7af69701fb3ef34497c66009dc1be031.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099170} {:answer/id #uuid "d811e88f-4ab8-5afc-a0ed-485346467342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42d7fe32912cf573862dcf1aaf191706efb7ff3188436281f67f6c49912e471f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099171} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6ea1c4-f91e-5f11-99d9-8f330ee938ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa6b7fa93e98c2d641a8026b6a4dc1a7d17c1c909da270deef29d63e64d0f5a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099172} {:answer/id #uuid "22fdd574-fe48-5460-bbcd-08be7a378a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/edad2e1d26b98973d4a888058da155510b31f009759db7f2b6707dc4bf5a6c50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099173} {:answer/id #uuid "3375239b-8f1e-5ebc-9151-0f79d24d5dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf9e9f8fa1d2bc0ec7036870102cdd50de260fa2aff23af3cbcf2678018f12aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099174}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ffe4290b-772c-5d6f-ba6e-07cc2478306b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099169}] :question/id #uuid "805cd960-0e6c-5413-ba15-d13411a05c8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20510" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e1d4dbd07dbf4e7f06b5ef2dd01ae2ef5fa82f3a5a78fb470388fad8b18af1e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y = f(x)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101309 :knowled